; stashvisit.eval - a level only VISITED, or only READ, is not stashed, so the
; next savemap does not rewrite it (code-review C308, C307). Judged by
; tools/EditorTest.py phase 31, on a scratch copy of dungeon-demo.
;
; 1. THE VISIT (C308). eval_arena -> crypt1 -> crypt2 -> crypt1, no edit
;    anywhere. Leaving a level used to stash its map whatever had happened to
;    it, and savemap writes every stash: crypt2.map came back rewritten (its
;    comments gone) though nobody had touched it. Each `stashes` must list no
;    map and no .ent, only the dynamic states the stairs keep, and savemap must
;    write crypt1 - the level stood on - alone.
; 2. THE QUERIES (C307). A dungeon delete ASKED (`dungeons what`) and one
;    started in the type editor and cancelled both walk every surviving level
;    for a stair leading in - and used to stash each one to read it. A fresh
;    dungeon with one empty level (dungeon1 / dungeon11) is the one whose
;    refusal gets that far: crypt and eval are refused before it (the party's
;    level, the opening, the harness level).
; 3. THE RENAME (C307). Renaming crypt2 repoints crypt1's stair (crypt1 is the
;    level stood on, so it is written at once), and every other level used to
;    be stashed just to look for a stair naming crypt2. Renamed back after.
; EditorTest compares crypt2's and eval_arena's files with the copy's as made:
; byte for byte, whatever the readouts said.

logecho on
reset
echo --- start ---
stashes
goto crypt1
goto crypt2
echo --- on crypt2 ---
stashes
goto crypt1
echo --- back ---
stashes
savemap
echo --- queries ---
newtype dungeons
levels new dungeon1
stashes
dungeons what dungeon1
dungeons dialog dungeon1
dungeons dialog delete
dungeons dialog off
echo --- after queries ---
stashes
echo --- rename ---
levelrename crypt2 crypt2b
stashes
levelrename crypt2b crypt2
echo --- renamed back ---
stashes
savemap
echo --- end ---
